{
  "meta": {
    "lastReviewed": "2026-08-04",
    "note": "Memory is the usable frame buffer or installed system RAM; bandwidth is the peak theoretical figure the vendor publishes, which is what bounds single-stream decoding. Unified devices share one pool between CPU and GPU, so only part of it can be handed to a model. Multi-device entries pool their memory — over NVLink inside a server, over PCIe in a desktop build — but each device still reads at the single-device bandwidth, which is why capacity scales with the device count and speed does not."
  },
